# Steam Deck packages that are NEVER BUNDLED -- fetched over the network at
# install time, onto the target, by configure_deck's `pkgs` step
# (T5-fork-plan.md §4.1).
#
# 🔴 CONSUMER, NAMED, BECAUSE ITS ABSENCE WAS THE BUG.
#
#   INSTALL TIME (the one that matters):
#     configs/airootfs/usr/share/omarchy-iso/orchestrator/deck_pkgs.py, running
#     inside `configure_deck` after pacstrap. It reads this file from
#     /usr/share/omarchy-iso/deck-fetch.packages on the LIVE ISO and runs
#     `arch-chroot <target> pacman -S --needed` over its entries against the
#     target's ONLINE repos. It records status + the pacman error tail in
#     /var/log/omarchy-deck-install.json and leaves a first-boot unit behind
#     when it could not finish.
#
#   BUILD TIME (asks about it; installs nothing):
#     deck-nvidia-dry-run.sh resolves `<what the ISO installs> + <this file>`
#     against the online repos, because docs/PROGRESS.md §3.8's NVIDIA
#     provider failure is a property of that COMBINED resolution and of nothing
#     the ISO carries on its own. Leaving these out of the question would make
#     the guard green for the wrong reason.
#     builder/build-iso.sh (via iso/overlay/patches/deck-packages.patch)
#     refuses the build if this file is empty, and COPIES it into
#     airootfs/usr/share/omarchy-iso/ -- without that copy the file exists only
#     inside the build container and the install-time consumer above cannot
#     see it at all. That copy is half of the P3.2 fix.
#
# From the day this file was written until 2026-08-15 the build-time reader was
# the ONLY reader. Nothing installed a single entry, the installed Deck had no
# Steam, gamescope came up with nothing to display, and the panel stayed black
# through two complete boots while eleven install steps reported green:
# docs/findings/P32-steam-never-installed.md.
#
# ⚠️ Adding a package here does NOT make it available offline, and an install
# with no network will not get it. If a package must be installable without a
# network it belongs in deck-mirror.packages + deck-install.packages -- and
# then its licence has to permit redistribution, which is the whole reason the
# one entry below is here instead.

# Steam Subscriber Agreement, and it is huge. Installers normally fetch it, and
# the installer's S0 screen discloses on screen that it is downloaded from
# Valve during setup. Bundling the client into the offline mirror would
# redistribute it; that is a licensing question this project has deliberately
# not taken on, so this stays a fetch even though its absence is the single
# most visible failure a Deck can have (no Steam = no Gaming Mode = black
# screen).
#
# It is also the package that makes §3.8 fire at all: `steam` is what depends
# on the virtual vulkan-driver / lib32-vulkan-driver that pacman would
# otherwise satisfy with the NVIDIA stack, which is why deck-install.packages
# names vulkan-radeon AND lib32-vulkan-radeon.
#
# Measured on hardware 2026-08-15, repairing the broken install in place:
# `pacman -S --needed steam` inside the target pulls 34 packages (steam
# 1.0.0.87-1 plus its lib32 runtime), all from multilib, with no kernel,
# bootloader or UKI involvement. Steam bootstrapped on the next boot and Gaming
# Mode rendered.
#
# ⚠️ `steamdeck-dsp` used to be the second entry here. It moved to
# deck-mirror.packages + deck-install.packages on 2026-08-15 (operator
# decision): it is firmware, the machine needs it to have working audio at all,
# and it now rides in the offline mirror the way linux-firmware-neptune always
# has. Do not move `steam` after it.
steam
